my agent usually sends me a script I  read it and if I'm interested then they  arrange a meeting with the director then  if I like the director and if the  director likes me we take it further we  discuss it we discuss the script any  notes I might have any suggestions for  rewrites or deletions and then if I'm  offered the job I usually go to the  read-through that's where all the actors  get together and all the heads of  department and we read the script and  again if there are any comments I give  them to the director and I usually start  the editing on the first day of  principal photography we set up the  editing rooms and then I get rushes the  following day a typical day in the  cutting room for me would be to watch  Russia's first thing in the morning at  around 9:30  my assistant usually starts at 6:30 so  that her name is Charlotte she can have  rushes for me right away i watch rushes  then I start assembling each scene and I  normally have to send the assembled  scenes to the director the same day so  my assemblies are very tightly cut  they've got soundtrack they've got sound  design if there are any green screens  I usually compose them first so that my  assistant can finesse them I layer all  the the compositions I want all the bits  and pieces if it's a visual effect I add  all the elements so I could have up to  ten elements sometimes and because it's  so time-consuming  my assistant Charlotte does them and  then we send it off at the end of the  day for the director once I've watched  the rushes I usually either call or text  the director to make them know that  everything's okay or if I have issues if  I want pickup shots or if they're  missing some of the subtext we discuss  it I like a very close relationship with  a director because I consider editing  the director's department they have to  trust me  we have to be in sync it doesn't mean we  have to agree on everything but we have  to have a fundamental understanding of  what we're trying to achieve and it's my  responsibility to make their vision come  across in the Edit well I like to work  with directors who can take a script and  elevate it and in the case of Stephen  Daldry  he creates magic he goes beyond the  script he he will insert moments and  details that no other directors will  think of so for me that's really  thrilling to get rushes and be  overwhelmed emotionally by them or laugh  out loud because they're so funny and  just directors who have good taste and  strong instincts are great for me I  originally wanted to be a fashion  photographer  I loved cameras I loved images and  interesting compositions I went to film  school and I studied still photography  and motion pictures and although I  operated a lot of a lot of films I kind  of fell into editing there was one  exercise in particular that really  resonated with me the whole class shot  one scene together and then we were  given the same rushes and most of my  friends cut in a wide shot too medium  shot to close-up and I chose to show an  image and prelab dialogue and start in  close-ups and then reveal the whole wide  shot so it was it was unconventional and  although I got top marks and praise for  my peers for me it was a turning point  so then when I finished school I ended  up as a clapper loader on a few  productions and on the the movie I was a  clapper loader on I just followed the  film through into the cutting room and  I've been there ever since  although the crown is considered  television for for Netflix  it's every episode is treated like a  feature film so for me I have the  collaboration with the director Steven  Daldry who is amazing and since he comes  from film he insists on keeping me on to  the end of the mix so as an editor I'm  able to follow the film through to the  very end  well I edited episodes 8 &amp; 9 8 deals  with the with the Kennedys coming to  Buckingham Palace and episode 9 is about  the Gordon Stone episode with Prince  Philip and the Prince Charles and their  experiences there now both episodes were  completely different in tone so I had  the pleasure of editing a comedy and  then a very serious heartbreaking drama  so for me I had the best of both worlds  in episode 9 although it was brilliantly  written I did restructure a lot of it  because there were times when we cut  away from one boy too early and  emotionally we wanted to stay with that  one boy whether it was Charles or Philip  I also had to find interesting  transitions and so visually it was it  was a challenge to to discover where we  should switch over what shots would  complement each other  for example after Prince Philip bullies  the Queen and insists that Charles stay  at Gordon stone we added one shot of  Philip going into the hallway and  looking out the window and then there's  just a flicker of remorse and he looks  down and script wise I was meant to cut  to young Phillip on the obstacle course  but I chose to move up a shot of Charles  young Charles looking in the trophy  cabinet at young Philip and so this  juxtaposition of older Phillip cutting  to young Charles looking at his father  as a young boy in the trophy cabinet  with the trophy was emotionally very  connecting of all three characters in  the show  so I try to apply that throughout the  whole show well we all dream of getting  great performances and I was really  blessed on the crown because from the  the big leads to the tiny parts gave  brilliant performances and I have to  credit Stephen Daldry for that because  in watching every take of his rushes an  actor might do something a few times and  then Stephen will go and whisper in  their ear and then the next take is  magically ten times better because  they'll do something it could it might  be subtle but they do something that  transforms that scene so I edited the  first season of the crown as well and I  was very excited to learn that handsomer  was scoring the film so I got all his  soundtracks from all the films he had  scored and we just and I think the other  editors too tried to use his sound and  music on as a temp track on the second  season of the crown we were even luckier  because we had the first season to draw  from musically the score had the right  tone for the show had some lovely  melodies so I was able to apply those  two to my shows as temp tracks but  Stephen Daldry and I were lucky because  we went to Rupert gregson-williams home  outside of London he has a beautiful  home with rose gardens and it was very  peaceful and we spent a day with him  working on episode 9 and he just played  some melodies that we knew would work  for the show so it was a close  relationship because Rupert would also  come to our cutting room and he would  bring some tracks and we would try them  and it was a very collaborative  relationship well I think there's a  misconception that the crown has tons of  money and we get tons of time and  everything but the reality is I was  editing two shows at the same time and  so I was working 14 16 hours a day  and usually six days a week I had the  luxury of having amazing material  starting from a great script and  material that Stephen Daldry had shot so  that was a blessing but when you work on  smaller budgets with less experienced  directors I would say it sometimes is  harder because you have little material  to play with and if a director is less  experienced they sometimes give you less  freedom to explore which is detrimental  to the film because it's very healthy to  have an unbiased editors opinion and  input into the film so I would say hire  the best people for the job and let them  do their their best and explore besides  being a storyteller an editor has to be  very sensitive to the director because  although they're very talented they too  can be vulnerable so if I have anything  to say I say it one-on-one and not in  front of the writer director I try to  help them out as much as possible so  after I've watched rushes I'll either  text email or call the director and  suggest pickup shots or pickup scenes if  I think we're missing a human and  emotional beat I think of the whole  character arc which is why I watch every  take because there could be a little gem  in an unselected take I put a soundtrack  I put temp music on the on the  assemblies so and I also have the  assistant create the visual effects  after I've put my comps in there so that  I try to deliver the most finished and  polished assembly possible so that the  director can really judge how it's  shaping up  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

